---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../site.config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Cyril Dettling — sixteen, from Schwyz, apprentice platform engineer, and the person who put KDE Plasma on Linux From Scratch."
>
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1 class="page-head__title">
        Sixteen, Swiss, and<br />far too invested in Linux
      </h1>
      <p class="page-head__intro">
        I build systems that can be thrown away and rebuilt exactly, and I got
        there by breaking a lot of systems that couldn't be.
      </p>
    </header>

    <hr class="rule" />

    <article class="entries">
      <section class="entry">
        <h2 class="label entry__label">Now</h2>
        <div class="entry__body prose">
          <p>
            I'm sixteen and I live in {site.author.location}. I'm currently doing
            my apprenticeship at the <strong>Kanton Schwyz</strong> as an
            <em>Informatiker EFZ, Fachrichtung Plattformentwicklung</em> — the
            platform engineering track, which in practice means servers,
            networks, virtualisation, and the infrastructure that everything else
            in an organisation quietly depends on.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's the rare case of the thing I'd be doing anyway turning out to be
            the thing I'm being trained in. Most of what I know, I learned by
            wanting something to work on my own machines first.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="entry">
        <h2 class="label entry__label">The Linux part</h2>
        <div class="entry__body prose">
          <p>
            I do unreasonable things with Linux. The clearest example: I built
            <strong>Linux From Scratch</strong> — the whole thing, compiled from
            source, no distribution underneath — and then, having reached the
            point where a sensible person stops, installed <strong>KDE
            Plasma</strong> on top of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you haven't tried it: LFS gives you a system that boots to a
            prompt and nothing else. Everything after that is yours to compile,
            in the right order, with the right flags, against the right versions.
            A full desktop environment is an enormous dependency graph to walk by
            hand. That was rather the point.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is not a practical way to run a computer, and I would not
            recommend it as one. But nothing else teaches you what a Linux system
            actually <em>is</em> quite as thoroughly as assembling one from
            nothing and watching it come up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="entry">
        <h2 class="label entry__label">Why Nix</h2>
        <div class="entry__body prose">
          <p>
            Building everything by hand taught me the shape of the problem, and
            the problem is that a hand-built system exists exactly once. Get it
            working, and you still can't reproduce it. Change something, and you
            can't reliably get back.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nix fixes precisely that, which is why nearly everything I've built
            since is Nix. My whole operating system is a
            <a class="link" href="/projects/nixos-dotfiles">flake in a repository</a>.
            My editor is
            <a class="link" href="/projects/nvim">a flake too</a>, pinned down to
            the plugin commit. My installer is
            <a class="link" href="/projects/nixos-images">an image built in CI</a>.
            And when all of that needed somewhere to cache its builds, I wrote
            <a class="link" href="/projects/aeroflare">Aeroflare</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            There's a straight line from compiling a desktop environment by hand
            to refusing to ever do it by hand again.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="entry">
        <h2 class="label entry__label">Elsewhere</h2>
        <div class="entry__body prose">
          <p>
            Most of what I make is public on
            <a class="link" href={`https://github.com/${site.author.github}`} rel="me noopener">GitHub</a>.
            If you want to reach me, the
            <a class="link" href="/contact">contact page</a> has every way to do it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .entries {
    display: grid;
  }

  /* Label sits in its own column and stays put while the prose scrolls past. */
  .entry {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-l);
    padding-block: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: var(--rule) solid var(--rule-color);
  }

  .entry__label {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--space-m);
    align-self: start;
    color: var(--fg-faint);
  }

  .entry__body {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .entry__body strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .entry {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-m);
      padding-block: var(--space-l);
    }

    .entry__label {
      position: static;
    }
  }
</style>
